{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GjCYa4zUoa39pNmHKhmv5b","type":"tool_result","content":"\t\t}\n\t\tvar stdout, stderr strings.Builder\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"init\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit = %d, want 0 (stderr: %q)\", code, stderr.String())\n\t\t}\n\t\tif opts.Version != \"dev\" {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"InitOpts.Version = %q, want dev\", opts.Version)\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\n\tt.Run(\"setup --check and labels sync reach their handlers\", func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\tvar setup []SetupOpts\n\t\tsynced := false\n\t\th := Handlers{\n\t\t\tSetup:      func(_ context.Context, _ *Env, o SetupOpts) error { setup = append(setup, o); return nil },\n\t\t\tLabelsSync: func(context.Context, *Env) error { synced = true; return nil },\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar stdout, stderr strings.Builder\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"setup\", \"--check\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"setup --check exit = %d (stderr %q)\", code, stderr.String())\n\t\t}\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"setup\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"setup exit = %d\", code)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(setup) != 2 || !setup[0].Check || setup[1].Check {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"SetupOpts = %+v\", setup)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"labels\", \"sync\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 || !synced {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"labels sync: exit %d, synced %v\", code, synced)\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\n\tt.Run(\"serve --loops reaches the handler\", func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\tvar got []string\n\t\th := Handlers{Serve: func(_ context.Context, _ *Env, o ServeOpts) error { got = o.Loops; return nil }}\n\t\tvar stdout, stderr strings.Builder\n// Package cli builds the ard command tree. Subcommand behaviour is\n// injected through Handlers; a nil field runs as a \"not implemented\"\n// stub until the issue that owns the command wires its handler in.\npackage cli\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\n\t\"github.com/spf13/cobra\"\n)\n\n// Globals holds the values of the global flags shared by every command.\ntype Globals struct {\n\tRepo       string // \"owner/repo\"; validated when set\n\tConfigPath string // default .ard/config.yaml in the target repo (#11)\n\tDryRun     bool\n\tLogLevel   string // debug|info|warn|error, default info\n}\n\n// Env is passed to every command handler.\n# Getting started\n\nThis takes a repository from nothing to a first triaged issue. Run the `ard` commands from a clone of the target repository, or pass `--repo owner/name`.\n\n## 1. Create and install the GitHub App\n\nard acts as a GitHub App, never as a personal token. Create an App you own and give it exactly these repository permissions:\n\n| Permission | Access |\n| --- | --- |\n| Contents | Read and write |\n| Issues | Read and write |\n| Pull requests | Read and write |\n| Checks | Read and write |\n| Commit statuses | Read |\n| Metadata | Read |\n\nDo **not** grant `workflows`. Without it GitHub itself rejects any push that edits `.github/workflows/`. Install the App on the target repository, then note the App ID and installation ID and download the private key (a `.pem` file; keep it mode 0600).\n\nGive `ard serve` the App through its environment: `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\n\n## 2. Let the App push to main\n\nard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`. If `main` has a ruleset, add the App to the ruleset's bypass list. Without that, pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n\n## 3. Audit the bot's commits\n\nSet the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n\n## 4. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init\n```\n\n`ard init` opens a pull request with the starter `.ard/` tree: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. It also writes `.ard/ard-init.lock`, the hash of each starter file it wrote. Review and merge it. Rerunning `ard init` after an upgrade updates only files you have not edited, never touches an edited file (it prints a diff against the starter instead) and never deletes. Then:\n\n```sh\nard labels sync\nard config check","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e831d755-0305-4792-92c3-10bc757b9430","uuid":"53ae60ab-5afc-479c-ac74-ea711eb2e0e5","timestamp":"2026-11-25T10:44:28.930Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\t\t}\n\t\tvar stdout, stderr strings.Builder\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"init\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit = %d, want 0 (stderr: %q)\", code, stderr.String())\n\t\t}\n\t\tif opts.Version != \"dev\" {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"InitOpts.Version = %q, want dev\", opts.Version)\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\n\tt.Run(\"setup --check and labels sync reach their handlers\", func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\tvar setup []SetupOpts\n\t\tsynced := false\n\t\th := Handlers{\n\t\t\tSetup:      func(_ context.Context, _ *Env, o SetupOpts) error { setup = append(setup, o); return nil },\n\t\t\tLabelsSync: func(context.Context, *Env) error { synced = true; return nil },\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar stdout, stderr strings.Builder\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"setup\", \"--check\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"setup --check exit = %d (stderr %q)\", code, stderr.String())\n\t\t}\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"setup\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"setup exit = %d\", code)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(setup) != 2 || !setup[0].Check || setup[1].Check {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"SetupOpts = %+v\", setup)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif code := Run([]string{\"labels\", \"sync\"}, &stdout, &stderr, h, \"dev\"); code != 0 || !synced {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"labels sync: exit %d, synced %v\", code, synced)\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\n\tt.Run(\"serve --loops reaches the handler\", func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\tvar got []string\n\t\th := Handlers{Serve: func(_ context.Context, _ *Env, o ServeOpts) error { got = o.Loops; return nil }}\n\t\tvar stdout, stderr strings.Builder\n// Package cli builds the ard command tree. Subcommand behaviour is\n// injected through Handlers; a nil field runs as a \"not implemented\"\n// stub until the issue that owns the command wires its handler in.\npackage cli\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\n\t\"github.com/spf13/cobra\"\n)\n\n// Globals holds the values of the global flags shared by every command.\ntype Globals struct {\n\tRepo       string // \"owner/repo\"; validated when set\n\tConfigPath string // default .ard/config.yaml in the target repo (#11)\n\tDryRun     bool\n\tLogLevel   string // debug|info|warn|error, default info\n}\n\n// Env is passed to every command handler.\n# Getting started\n\nThis takes a repository from nothing to a first triaged issue. Run the `ard` commands from a clone of the target repository, or pass `--repo owner/name`.\n\n## 1. Create and install the GitHub App\n\nard acts as a GitHub App, never as a personal token. Create an App you own and give it exactly these repository permissions:\n\n| Permission | Access |\n| --- | --- |\n| Contents | Read and write |\n| Issues | Read and write |\n| Pull requests | Read and write |\n| Checks | Read and write |\n| Commit statuses | Read |\n| Metadata | Read |\n\nDo **not** grant `workflows`. Without it GitHub itself rejects any push that edits `.github/workflows/`. Install the App on the target repository, then note the App ID and installation ID and download the private key (a `.pem` file; keep it mode 0600).\n\nGive `ard serve` the App through its environment: `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\n\n## 2. Let the App push to main\n\nard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`. If `main` has a ruleset, add the App to the ruleset's bypass list. Without that, pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n\n## 3. Audit the bot's commits\n\nSet the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n\n## 4. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init\n```\n\n`ard init` opens a pull request with the starter `.ard/` tree: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. It also writes `.ard/ard-init.lock`, the hash of each starter file it wrote. Review and merge it. Rerunning `ard init` after an upgrade updates only files you have not edited, never touches an edited file (it prints a diff against the starter instead) and never deletes. Then:\n\n```sh\nard labels sync\nard config check","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GjCYa4zUoa39pNmHKhmv5b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
